---
import PostLayout from "../../layouts/PostLayout.astro";

const content = `
<p>
  To improve traceability of documentation commits in Perforce, we needed a structured changelist
  description template for the team. The standard approach is to use P4 triggers, but our IT team
  had restricted trigger usage on the Perforce server. We needed a client-side alternative.
</p>

<h2>The Solution</h2>

<p>
  A colleague wrote a client-side Python script using the <code>p4python</code> library that
  creates a new numbered Perforce changelist with a pre-filled description template.
</p>

<pre><code>import ctypes
from P4 import P4

def main():
    p4obj = P4()
    p4obj.client = 'user_workspace'
    p4obj.connect()
    if not p4obj.connected():
        ctypes.windll.user32.MessageBoxW(
            0, "Unable to Connect", "Error", 0)
        return

    change = p4obj.fetch_change()
    change._description = """
    Fix for STAR:
    Release (LCA/GA/version):
    Customer Name?
    Section that is changed in the source:
    Creating PDF?
    PDF Edition Number?
    Technical Review Done?
    """

    ctypes.windll.user32.MessageBoxW(
        0, *p4obj.save_change(change), "New P4 Change", 0)
    p4obj.disconnect()

if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()</code></pre>

<h2>Prerequisites</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Python 3 installed with environment variables configured</li>
  <li><code>p4python</code> installed: <code>pip3 install p4python</code></li>
</ul>

<h2>How It Works</h2>

<ol>
  <li>The script connects to the Perforce server using the specified client workspace</li>
  <li>It fetches a new changelist and populates the description with a structured template</li>
  <li>The changelist is saved and the changelist number is displayed in a dialog box</li>
  <li>Team members double-click the script to create a new changelist with the template pre-filled</li>
</ol>

<h2>Why This Matters</h2>

<p>
  Structured changelist descriptions improve documentation change traceability. When every commit
  includes the release version, review status, and affected sections, it becomes much easier to
  audit documentation changes during release cycles. The client-side approach works around server
  restrictions without requiring IT involvement.
</p>
`;
---

<PostLayout
  title="Customizing Changelist Description in Perforce"
  date="2022"
  summary="A client-side Python script to add structured changelist templates in Perforce when server-side triggers are restricted."
>
  <Fragment set:html={content} />
</PostLayout>
